{
  "example": "In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. {cite}`requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Two tests carry `{test}` references to the example, and together they cover its scenario and both expected selectors.\n\n- **Scenario setup:**\n  - `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry` (src/outline/tests.rs:35-100) parses the source with the path `src/lib.rs`. That source contains `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }`.\n  - `selectors_name_syntactic_ancestry_and_duplicates` (src/outline/tests.rs:657-701) uses the same line, with the path `lib.rs`.\n- **Nested selector `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`:** Both tests assert that this selector is among the outline's selectors. The first test also asserts that `outline.resolve(\"fn[outer]/fn[inner]\")` returns a node whose selector equals that string.\n- **Top-level selector `fn[outer]`:** No test lists `fn[outer]` on its own, but the nested assertions cover it. `Outline::resolve` (src/outline/mod.rs:851-867) walks one segment at a time from the root. Resolving the nested selector therefore requires a root-level child with segment `fn[outer]`. `disambiguate` (src/outline/mod.rs:668-696) builds a nested selector as `parent.selector + \"/\" + segment`, and a root-level selector is just its segment. So the nested selector can exist only if a top-level node with selector `fn[outer]` exists as its parent. A separate test, `line_queries_find_innermost_nodes` (src/outline/tests.rs:448-470), asserts `fn[outer]` directly. It carries only a `requirements.references.outlines` reference, not one to this example, so I did not rely on it.\n\nNo gaps found.",
  "references": "f3c49019e423d681f14511a10dd0ec04ee48b588885830e2e0fe64fed09ff45b",
